AB  - The cell wall is crucial for fungal growth, proliferation and interaction with the environment and host. Understanding the regulatory mechanism of cell wall integrity may help with improvement of fungal biocontrol agents. Here, a putative target of the cell wall integrity pathway-involved Slt2 MAP kinase, Mb1, an orthologue of MADS-box transcription factor Rlm1, was characterized in an economically important insect fungal pathogen, Beauveria bassiana. Mb1 disruption mutant (Mb1) displayed reduced growth and increased conidial production on minimal medium but not on rich-nutrient media, which is different from Slt2 to a great extent. Loss of Mb1 resulted in a significant increase in sensitivity to cell wall-perturbing agents (Congo red and calcofluor white), with alteration in cell composition that was inconsistent with Slt2 strain, including increased chitin content and reduced chitin-binding -1, 3/1,6-glucan levels in the absence of any stress. Transcription levels of 15 chitin synthesis and metabolism-associated and 17 Pkc1-Slt2 CWI (cell wall integrity) pathway, glucan synthesis, and cell wall remodeling enzyme synthesis-involved genes were significantly increased and repressed in Mb1 strain, respectively, some of which were verified to be the targets of Mb1. Insect bioassays revealed decreased virulence for the Mb1 strain in both topical and intrahemocoel injection assays. Our results demonstrated that Mb1 control fungal biocontrol potential-associated traits, inclu
